Amazon SNS — Data Limitations

Scope: Constraints on message data for application-to-application use of Amazon SNS. Excludes throughput quotas and A2P channels (SMS, email, push).
Last verified: September 2026, against the AWS General Reference and SNS Developer Guide.

1. Payload size
Limit	Value
Maximum message size	262,144 bytes (256 KiB)
Maximum header size	16,384 bytes (16 KiB)
Extended payload maximum	2 GB
Billing unit	64 KB per request

The 256 KiB limit is measured in bytes, not characters. Multi-byte UTF-8 characters consume 2–4 bytes each, so payloads within limits by character count may still be rejected.

The 2 GB maximum is not native to SNS. It is provided by the Extended Client Libraries, which store the payload in S3 and publish a pointer — every subscriber must use the corresponding library to resolve it.

Billing is per 64 KB chunk, applied to both publishes and deliveries. A 256 KB message is billed as four requests on each.

2. Encoding and content
Messages must be UTF-8 encoded strings. Permitted content: XML, JSON, or unformatted text.
Binary is not supported in the message body. It must be base64-encoded first, adding roughly 33% to size against the 256 KiB limit.
Binary is supported natively only as a message attribute with DataType: Binary.
3. Message attributes

Optional structured metadata sent alongside — but separate from — the body.

Constraint	Value
Counts toward message size	Yes — name, type, and value included in the 256 KiB total
Supported data types	String, String.Array, Number, Binary
Maximum count (SQS with Raw Message Delivery)	10
Maximum count (all other cases)	Bounded only by the 256 KiB size limit

Critical behaviors

Attributes are transmitted only when MessageStructure is String, not JSON.
For SQS subscriptions with Raw Message Delivery enabled, messages carrying more than 10 attributes are discarded as client-side errors with no failure indication to the publisher. Disable raw delivery to send more.
When an attribute is used for filtering, its value must be a valid JSON string.
Name, type, and value must each be non-empty and non-null.
4. Structural field limits
Field	Constraint
Topic name	1–256 characters; ASCII letters, numbers, underscores, hyphens only; FIFO must end in .fifo
Subject	UTF-8, under 100 characters, no line breaks or control characters
MessageGroupId (FIFO)	Max 128 characters; required on every message — publish fails if omitted
MessageDeduplicationId (FIFO)	Max 128 characters; required unless ContentBasedDeduplication is enabled
Batch entry Id	Max 80 characters, unique within the batch
Messages per PublishBatch	10 entries

When MessageStructure is json, the object must contain a default key, and protocol keys must map to simple JSON string values. Nested objects are not permitted at the top level.

5. Filter policy limits
Constraint	Value
Maximum policy size	256 KB
Maximum keys per policy	5
Maximum combination of values	150
Numeric matching range	-1,000,000,000 to 1,000,000,000
Numeric precision	5 decimal places
Wildcard complexity ceiling	100 points
Policies per topic / per account	200 / 10,000 (both adjustable)

Total combination of values is calculated by multiplying the number of values in each array. String matching is case-sensitive. Numeric values outside the supported range fail to match silently rather than raising an error. The policy must be valid UTF-8 JSON containing only quoted strings, numbers, and the unquoted keywords true, false, null.

The per-topic limit does not block subscription creation; the call fails only when the policy is attached via Subscribe or SetSubscriptionAttributes.

6. Data retention

Standard topics retain nothing. There is no storage, no replay, and no consumer polling. A message exists only for the duration of its delivery attempts, then is discarded.

Endpoint type	Retry budget before discard
SQS, Lambda	100,015 attempts over 23 days
HTTP/S	Customer-defined; total retry time capped at 3,600 seconds
SMTP, SMS, mobile push	50 attempts over 6 hours

If all attempts fail and no dead-letter queue is attached, the message is permanently lost. DLQs attach at the subscription level, not the topic level.

FIFO topics are the sole exception: archive and replay policies retain messages for a configurable window and allow replay to subscriptions. No equivalent exists for standard topics.

7. Encryption and deduplication
Server-side encryption via AWS KMS, using an AWS managed or customer managed key.
Data protection policies can scan and redact PII in transit. PutDataProtectionPolicy is throttled to 1 TPS in all commercial regions.
Content-based deduplication on FIFO topics hashes the body only, excluding attributes. Two messages with identical bodies and different attributes are treated as duplicates and only one is delivered.
8. Design implications
Constraint	Implication
No retention on standard topics	Attach a DLQ to every subscription carrying data you cannot lose
At-least-once delivery, no ordering	Consumers must be idempotent and tolerate out-of-order arrival
256 KiB ceiling	Use a claim-check pattern (S3 pointer) for larger payloads unless all consumers can adopt the Extended Client Library
Silent discard above 10 attributes	Validate attribute count before publish — failures are not surfaced
64 KB billing chunks	Payload size is a cost lever, not just a technical limit
Body-only dedup hash	Do not rely on attributes to differentiate otherwise-identical FIFO messages

References: AWS General Reference — SNS endpoints and quotas; SNS Developer Guide — message attributes, filter policy constraints, message delivery retries.
